@@ -0,0 +1,127 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+Covers `GhostPreview.server.luau`, `GhostPreview.client.luau` and `Shared/GhostProtocol.luau`.
+Block placement (`PlaceBlock`, `BlockPlacer`, `BlockPlacement.server.luau`) is unchanged.
+
+## What changed
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Client sends | `MoveGhost(name, cell)` every `RenderStepped`, even when nothing changed or placing is off (`nil, nil`) | `MoveGhost(state)` only when the cell or block type changes, at most 20 times a second |
+| Payload | a string and a `Vector3` | a 7-byte buffer: block type index (u8) and grid cell (3 × i16) |
+| Server does | moves a replicated ghost `Part` per message, which Roblox replicates to every client | keeps the latest state per player; every 1/20 s sends the ones that changed to all clients in **one** batched `GhostUpdates` event (15 bytes per ghost) |
+| Your own ghost | the server's part, so it lags your cursor by a full round trip | drawn locally every frame, so no network delay |
+| Other ghosts | jump cell to cell whenever a property update arrives | client parts that glide toward the latest cell (exponential smoothing, 40 ms time constant) |
+| Late joiners | see parts already in Workspace | call `GetGhosts` once to get every current ghost |
+
+## Assumptions
+
+Roblox doesn't publish its exact wire format, so these are round numbers. The conclusions hold
+across a wide range of values because the big change is in message *counts*.
+
+- **H ≈ 20 bytes**: overhead per remote event call (remote reference, framing, packet headers
+  shared across calls).
+- Argument sizes: a short string ≈ 2 + length bytes, a `Vector3` ≈ 13 bytes, a buffer ≈ 2 + length
+  bytes, `nil` ≈ 1 byte.
+- **R ≈ 25 bytes**: one replicated `CFrame` change on an anchored part (instance reference,
+  property id, position, compressed rotation). Setting a property to the value it already has
+  sends nothing, so before the change the server→client cost only came from *cell changes*, not
+  from every message.
+- **C** = how many times a second a builder's cursor moves into a different cell. Slow aiming is
+  0–5; sweeping across the ground or turning the camera is 15–30+. It is capped by frame rate.
+- **N** = players, **F** = client frame rate.
+
+## Per-message sizes
+
+- Before, client → server: H + (2 + 5 for `"Brick"`) + 13 ≈ **40 B** (≈ 22 B for `nil, nil`).
+- Before, server → each client: R ≈ **25 B** per cell change per builder.
+- After, client → server: H + 2 + 7 ≈ **29 B**.
+- After, server → each client: H + ~4 (buffer length + u16 count) + 15 × D ≈ **24 + 15·D B**, where
+  D is how many ghosts changed during that 1/20 s.
+
+## Formulas
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Upload per player | F × 40 B/s, all the time | min(C, 20) × 29 B/s, 0 when idle |
+| Messages into server | N × F per second | Σ min(C, 20) ≤ 20·N per second |
+| Download per player | Σ over builders of C × 25 B/s | ≤ 20 × (24 + 15·D) B/s, 0 when no ghost changes |
+
+## Scenario 1: the playtest (12 players, 4 building at C ≈ 15, average 144 FPS)
+
+**Before**
+
+- Upload per player: 144 × 40 = **5,760 B/s ≈ 5.8 KB/s** (46 kbit/s), all 12 players, including the
+  8 who aren't building, because the loop fires every frame no matter what.
+- Into the server: 12 × 144 = **1,728 messages/s**, 1,728 × 40 ≈ **69 KB/s**, each one running the
+  handler and writing four part properties.
+- Download per player: 4 × 15 × 25 = **1.5 KB/s** across 60 property updates/s.
+
+**After**
+
+- Upload per builder: 15 × 29 = **435 B/s**; the 8 others send **0**.
+- Into the server: 4 × 15 = **60 messages/s**, ≈ **1.7 KB/s**.
+- Download per player: 60 changes/s spread over 20 batches gives D ≈ 3, so
+  20 × (24 + 45) ≈ **1.4 KB/s** across **20** messages/s.
+
+## Scenario 2: worst case (12 players all sweeping at C ≈ 30, 240 FPS)
+
+**Before**
+
+- Upload per player: 240 × 40 = **9.6 KB/s** (77 kbit/s).
+- Into the server: 12 × 240 = **2,880 messages/s**, ≈ **115 KB/s**.
+- Download per player: 12 × 30 × 25 = **9 KB/s**, 360 property updates/s.
+
+**After**
+
+- Upload per player: 20 × 29 = **580 B/s** (sends are capped at 20/s).
+- Into the server: 12 × 20 = **240 messages/s**, ≈ **7 KB/s**.
+- Download per player: 20 × (24 + 12 × 15) = **4.1 KB/s**, 20 messages/s.
+
+## Scenario 3: everyone in the game, nobody moving their cursor between cells
+
+- Before: still N × F messages a second, about 1,728/s at 12 players and 144 FPS.
+- After: **0**.
+
+## Summary
+
+| 12 players | Before (S1 / S2) | After (S1 / S2) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Upload per player | 5.8 / 9.6 KB/s | 0–0.44 / 0.58 KB/s |
+| Messages into server | 1,728 / 2,880 per s | 60 / 240 per s |
+| Bytes into server | 69 / 115 KB/s | 1.7 / 7 KB/s |
+| Download per player | 1.5 / 9 KB/s | 1.4 / 4.1 KB/s |
+| Messages to each player | 60 / 360 per s | 20 / 20 per s |
+
+Server inbound load drops by roughly 12–30×, and client upload by 13–17× while building (to zero
+otherwise). Download goes down less in the light case. It already only cost cell changes, but it
+now has a hard ceiling of 20 messages a second however many people build, and it grows by 15 B
+per extra active builder per batch. At 24 players all sweeping it would be about
+20 × (24 + 360) ≈ 7.7 KB/s.
+
+Why the ping went to 300–400 ms: the most likely cause is the per-frame upload. Remote events
+are reliable and ordered, so at 144–240 events a second each client's outgoing queue and the
+server's ~2,000–3,000 handler calls a second delay everything that shares those channels,
+character movement included. Ghosts jumped because replicated property updates for an anchored
+part arrive in uneven bursts and nothing smooths them. Both causes are gone now. These figures are
+estimates. Check them with the network stats in the Developer Console (F9) on a real server with
+12 players.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Your own ghost is snappier.** It follows the cursor on the same frame instead of a round trip
+  later.
+- **Other players' ghosts glide instead of teleporting.** They slide between cells over ~100 ms
+  rather than snapping, so a ghost briefly sits between two cells while it moves.
+- **Other players' ghosts trail a little further behind.** Added delay on top of normal latency:
+  up to 50 ms waiting for the client's send slot, up to 50 ms for the server's batch, and ~40 ms of
+  smoothing, so 50–150 ms in total. In the old setup the ping spikes caused far more delay than
+  this, but on a quiet server the new one is slightly behind.
+- **Fast sweeps skip cells.** When a builder crosses more than 20 cells a second, others see the
+  ghost glide straight toward the latest cell instead of touching every one in between. Jumps of
+  more than 48 studs snap instead of gliding.
+- **Ghosts no longer exist on the server.** `Workspace.Ghosts` is now a client-only folder, so the
+  server view in Studio doesn't show ghosts and server code can't read them. Nothing in the
+  project did.
+- **Very far cells aren't shared.** Cells more than about ±131,000 studs from the origin show for
+  their builder but are sent as hidden (the i16 grid limit). The baseplate is 2,048 studs wide.
